Is/features Option 2: Treating case by case pending on test cases introduced For NR Rel-16 V2X performance requirements, we can introduce two methods on the performance requirements introduction: Option 1: Create new sub-sections for different physical channels and SDR introduced for NR V2X under section 5, such as PSCCH, PSSCH, PSFCH and PSBCH, that are parallel with existing PDSCH, PDCCH and PBCH; create new sub-section for CSI if related performance requirements agreed under section 6: Pros: Only extend the Heading 2 sections, no further Heading 1 section creation. Cons: Too many Heading 2 sub-sections, scatter the performance requirements of V2X into different sub-sections, it is harder for reader to understand the specification for the whole V2X performance. Option 2: In chapter 5, create new sub-sections (e.g. section 5.6) for demodulation part of sidelink. In chapter 6, create a new section (i.e. section 6.5) for CSI part of sidelink if necessary: Pros: It is clearer for reader to distinguish the V2X performance requirements from the specification. Adapt to the existing specification structure as much as possible as per Demodulation performance requirements CSI reporting requirements Cons: Maybe not feasible for other Rel-16 WI performance, especially those Rel-16 performance requirements defined for Rel-15 features. Option 3: Create a new Heading 1 chapter (i.e. Chapter 11) for demodulation requirements part of sidelink. Create a new Heading 1 chapter (i.e. Chapter 12) for CSI reporting part of sidelink. Pros: It is clearer for reader to understand the NR V2X performance requirements. Cons: More Heading 1 sections will be created if other Rel-16 WIs follow the same principle. Option 4: Create a new Heading 1 chapter (i.e. Chapter 11) for all performance requirements of NR V2X including separate sub-sections for demodulation performance requirements and CSI reporting requirements if necessary: Pros: It is clearer for reader to understand the NR V2X performance requirements, reduce the number of Heading 1 sections in the specification, and converge all NR V2X performance requirements. Cons: Separate section for NR V2X performance requirements. By looking through Option 1 ~ Option 4 listed above, we think that Option 2 and Option 4 are the feasible ways to go for introduction of NR V2X performance requirements into TS 36.101-4 by considering the whole specification structure, but maybe Option 4 is better. Proposal 1: Create a new Heading 1 section (such as chapter 11) for NR V2X performance requirements as shown below: In this paper we give our discussions on spec structure for Rel-16 V2X. . Proposal 1: Create a new Heading 1 section (such as chapter 11) for NR V2X performance requirements as shown below: R4-1805548, WF on 38.101-4 spec skeleton, Samsung, Intel, Huawei Table 1: 5 Demodulation performance requirements (Conducted requirements) 5.1 General 5.2 PDSCH demodulation requirements 5.3 PDCCH demodulation requirements 5.4 PBCH demodulation requirements 5.5 Sustained downlink data rate provided by lower layer

{"chunk_id":"R4-2514518-007","collection":"ran4_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["CATT"],"tdocNumber":"R4-2514518","title":"Topic Summary for [116bis][111] 6G operation efficiency"},"point_id":"13727375487862109354"}
s Option 2: different sub-specs for common features and other vertical UE features (e.g. sidelink, NTN) Option 3: different sub-specs for core, performance, TC separately. Proposal 3: The basic functionalities and prioritize 6G day-1 typical cases’ requirements. Proposal 4: Avoid duplication and repetition of UE requirements for different scenarios and use cases. Proposal 5: Proposal 5a: RAN4 to study how new features introduced in 6G are captured in RRM requirements specification. Proposal 5b: New features introduced in later releases are captured either by incorporating in existing sections or by creating new sub-sections. Same suffix should be used for the same feature in different sub-sections. Proposal 6: Proposal 6a: Consider split RRM spec into two files for core part and performance part, respectively. Proposal 6b: Avoid defining too huge spec like 38.133; consider splitting the 6G RRM spec into several specs covering core requirements, performance requirements, and test cases respectively. Recommended WF To be further discussed Issue 3-2-3: Drafting approach Proposals Proposal 1: Adopt RAN2 pseudo-code approach in all sections. Proposal 2: Uses block-based approach to define core requirements and test cases. Proposal 3: A new tool, if possible, is used to capture tabulated test setup in test cases. Recommended WF To be further discussed Issue 3-2-4: Readability and simplification Proposals Proposal 1: Study how to reduce the amount of RRM procedure and requirements are defined only for key RRM procedures. Proposal 2: Proposal 2a: Include references or mapping tables in the core part requirements that point to the relevant test cases in 6G. Proposal 2b: Include a reference to the corresponding test cases in the corresponding requirement clause (e.g., in the text or as a new sub-clause). Proposal 2c: Test cases for specific applications or use cases can be in a separate section, but without breaking the main structure and mapping between core requirements and test cases: Option 1: use cases added as separate sections after the main test case hierarchy. Option 2: use cases grouped at a second level of the test case hierarchy. Proposal 2d: If common configurations can be identified for different test cases, they can be collected in a common section (e.g. under A.3 RRM test configurations or similar). Proposal 3: Reduce redundancy by referencing common descriptions instead of repeating descriptions in multiple places. Proposal 4: Editorial modifications in NR can be used as the baseline for future optimization toward 6G. Recommended WF To be further discussed

